\h 4 Maccabees
\toc1 The Fourth Document about the Maccabees
\toc2 4 Maccabees
\toc3 4Ma
\mt2 The fourth document about
\mt1 the Maccabees
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk The Fourth Document about the Maccabees\bk* is a sustained argument about faith, clear thinking, and self-control. The writer asks whether reason guided by devotion to God can govern desires and emotions such as greed, anger, fear, and pain. The point isn't that faithful people feel nothing, but that they can choose to obey God's instructions even when their feelings pull them strongly in another direction.
\ip After explaining this argument and giving examples, the writer retells the suffering of Eleazar, seven brothers, and their mother under King Antiokos (Antiochus). These are the people whose deaths are also described in chapters 6–7 of \bk The Second Document about the Maccabees\bk*. Here their speeches and endurance become the main examples supporting the argument.
\ip The closing chapters dwell on the brothers' love for one another and their mother's love for them. The writer praises their faithfulness, presents their endurance as a victory over the king's cruelty, and encourages readers to follow their devotion to God. This is a reflection on the meaning of their suffering, with the argument and the account woven together throughout.
\ip \bk The Fourth Document about the Maccabees\bk* appears in an appendix to the Greek Septuagint. It is considered to be apocrypha by most church traditions. It is preserved here for its supplementary historical value.
\ip Makabi (from which the term Maccabee is derived) is an acronym formed from the first letters of Exodus 15:11: “Mi kamokha ba'elim YHWH” — “Who is like you among the gods, Yahweh?”
\iot Main components of this document
\io1 How faithful reasoning can govern the emotions \ior 1:1–3:18\ior*
\io1 The background to Antiokos' persecution \ior 3:19–4:26\ior*
\io1 Eleazar's refusal, suffering, and example \ior 5:1–7:25\ior*
\io1 The seven brothers remain faithful through torture and death \ior 8:1–12:20\ior*
\io1 The brothers' unity and their mother's courage \ior 13:1–17:24\ior*
\io1 The closing appeal and the family's teaching \ior 18:1-24\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Faith and reason can govern our emotions
\p
\v 1 As I'm going to¦97666 demonstrate a most philosophical proposition, namely, that¦97679 religious reasoning is absolute¦97668 master¦97668 of the emotions. I'd willingly advise you¦97677 to give the¦97682 utmost heed to philosophy¦97683.
\v 2 Because¦97685 reason is necessary¦97686 to everyone¦97689 as a step to science. In addition¦97692, it embraces the¦97694 praise¦97702 of self-control, the highest virtue.
\v 3 If¦97703, then, reasoning appears¦97711 to hold the mastery over the emotions which stand in the way of temperance, such as gluttony and lust,
\v 4 it surely also and¦97718 manifestly rules over the affections which are contrary to¦97723 justice, such¦97726 as¦97726 malice¦97727, and¦97728 of those which are hindrances to¦97733 courage, such as anger¦97734, pain, and fear¦97739.
\v 5 Perhaps some may ask, “How is it, then, that reasoning, if it rules the emotions, isn't also master of forgetfulness and ignorance?” They attempt a ridiculous argument.
\v 6 Because reasoning doesn't rule over its own emotions, but over those that are contrary to justice, courage, temperance, and self-control; and yet over these, so as to withstand, without destroying them.
\p
\v 7 I might prove¦97798 to you from many other considerations, that¦97799 religious reasoning is¦97801 sole master of the emotions;
\v 8 but¦97808 I'll prove it¦97810 with the greatest¦97809 force from¦97812 the fortitude of Eleazar, and seven¦97822 kindred, and¦97824 their mother¦97827, who suffered death in defence of virtue¦97817.
\v 9 Because all¦97828 these, treating pains with contempt even to death¦97833, by this contempt, demonstrated that¦97838 reasoning has command over the emotions.
\v 10 Because their virtues, then, it's right that I should commend those men who died with their mother at this time on behalf of nobility and goodness; and for their honours, I may count them blessed.
\v 11 Because they, winning admiration not only from men in general, but even from the persecutors, for their courage and endurance, became the means of the destruction of the tyranny against their nation, having conquered the tyrant by their endurance, so that by them their country was purified.
\v 12 But¦97908 we may now¦97912 at once enter on the¦97918 question, having commenced, as¦97920 is our custom, with laying down the doctrine, and so¦97914 proceed to the¦97926 account of these people, giving¦97932 glory¦97931 to¦97932 the¦97933 all-wise God¦97935.
\s1 Reason, wisdom, and the emotions
\p
\v 13 Therefore the question whether reasoning is¦97941 absolute master of the¦97943 emotions.
\v 14 Let's determine, then¦97948, what reasoning is¦97949 and¦97951 what emotion is, and¦97954 how¦97955 many¦97955 forms of emotion there are, and¦97958 whether reasoning rules over all¦97960 of these.
\v 15 Reasoning is¦97969 intellect accompanied by a life¦97973 of righteousness, putting foremost the¦97975 consideration of wisdom¦97976.
\v 16 Wisdom¦97978 is¦97981 a knowledge¦97982 of divine and¦97984 human¦97985 things¦97986, and¦97987 of their causes.
\v 17 This¦97991 is¦97994 contained in the¦97995 education of¦97996 the¦97996 law¦97997, by means of¦97999 which¦98000 we learn divine things reverently and¦98004 human things profitably.
\v 18 The¦98009 forms of wisdom¦98011 are self-control, justice, courage¦98018, and¦98019 temperance.
\v 19 The¦98023 leading one of these is self-control, by whose means, indeed, it's that reasoning rules over the emotions.
\v 20 Of the emotions, pleasure¦98040 and¦98034 pain are¦98036 the¦98037 two¦98039 most comprehensive; and they also by nature refer to the¦98049 soul¦98050.
\v 21 There are many attendant affections surrounding pleasure¦98057 and¦98058 pain.
\v 22 Before¦98064 pleasure is¦98069 lust; and after¦98071 pleasure¦98074, joy¦98075.
\v 23 Before¦98076 pain is¦98080 fear¦98081; and after¦98082 pain is sorrow.
\v 24 Anger¦98087 is an affection, common¦98089 to¦98091 pleasure and¦98093 to pain, if¦98095 any one¦98097 will pay attention when¦98098 it¦98099 comes on¦98100 him.
\v 25 There exists in¦98101 pleasure¦98104 a malicious disposition¦98109, which is the most complex of all¦98111 the¦98112 affections.
\v 26 In the soul¦98117, it's arrogance, love¦98120 of¦98120 money¦98120, thirst for honour, contention, faithlessness, and the evil eye.
\v 27 In the¦98130 body¦98131, it's greediness, indiscriminate¦98132 eating¦98132, and solitary gluttony.
\p
\v 28 As¦98137 pleasure and pain are, therefore¦98138, two growths out of¦98140 the¦98140 body¦98141 and¦98142 the¦98143 soul¦98144, so there are¦98155 many offshoots of these emotions.
\v 29 Reasoning, the¦98159 universal farmer, purging and pruning each¦98158 of these, tying up, watering, and transplanting, in every¦98171 way¦98172 improves the¦98175 materials of the¦98176 morals and¦98178 affections.
\v 30 Because reasoning is the¦98181 leader of the¦98184 virtues, but it's¦98187 the sole ruler¦98188 of the¦98189 emotions.
\p Observe then¦98195 first¦98196, through¦98197 the very things which stand in the way of temperance, that¦98203 reasoning is absolute¦98204 ruler of the emotions.
\v 31 Now temperance consists of a command over the¦98215 lusts.
\v 32 But¦98218 of the¦98220 lusts, some belong to¦98222 the¦98224 soul and others to the¦98230 body. Reasoning appears¦98233 to¦98233 rule over both¦98229.
\v 33 Otherwise, how¦98235 is it that¦98247 when urged on to¦98237 forbidden meats, we reject the gratification which would come from¦98243 them? Isn't it because reasoning is¦98248 able¦98248 to command the appetites? I¦98254 believe so.
\v 34 Hence it is, then¦98260, that when¦98262 craving seafood, birds, four-footed¦98263 animals, and all kinds of food¦98265 which are forbidden to¦98269 us by the¦98270 law¦98271, we withhold ourselves through¦98273 the¦98274 mastery of¦98275 reasoning.
\v 35 Because¦98279 the affections of our appetites are resisted by the¦98285 temperate understanding, and bent back again, and all¦98291 the¦98292 impulses of¦98293 the¦98293 body¦98294 are reined in by¦98296 reasoning.
\c 2
\s1 Controlling desire and anger
\p
\v 1 Is it any wonder? If¦98302 the¦98303 lusts of the¦98304 soul¦98305, after participation with¦98307 what's beautiful, are frustrated,
\v 2 on this¦98313 ground, therefore, the¦98315 temperate Yosef \add !(Joseph)\add* is praised in that¦98319 by reasoning, he subdued, on reflection, the¦98320 indulgence of the¦98324 senses.
\v 3 Because, although young, and¦98329 ripe for¦98327 sexual intercourse, he nullified by reasoning the stimulus of his emotions.
\v 4 It isn't merely the stimulus of sensual indulgence, but¦98351 that of every¦98353 desire¦98354, that reasoning is able to master.
\v 5 For example, the¦98357 law¦98358 says¦98355, “You¦98365 mustn't¦98366 desire your neighbour's¦98369 wife¦98362, nor anything that¦98368 belongs to¦98371 your neighbour.”
\v 6 Now, then¦98373, since it's the¦98378 law¦98379 which has forbidden us to¦98381 desire¦98387, I'll much¦98380 the more easily persuade you¦98384, that¦98385 reasoning is¦98389 able¦98389 to govern our lusts, just¦98392 as¦98392 it does the affections which are impediments to¦98398 justice.
\v 7 Since¦98399 in what way¦98401 is¦98404 a solitary eater, a glutton, and a drunkard¦98410 reclaimed, unless it's clear¦98414 that¦98415 reasoning is lord¦98416 of the emotions?
\v 8 Therefore, a man¦98429 who regulates his course by the¦98424 law¦98425, even¦98427 if¦98427 he's¦98430 a lover of money, immediately puts pressure on his own disposition by lending to¦98431 the needy without¦98438 interest, and¦98440 cancelling the¦98441 debt¦98442 on the¦98443 seventh year.
\v 9 If¦98447 a man¦98449 is¦98450 greedy, he's ruled by¦98451 the¦98452 law¦98453 acting through¦98455 reasoning, so that he doesn't glean his harvest crops or vintage. In reference to¦98467 other¦98469 points we may perceive that¦98473 it's¦98476 reasoning that conquers his emotions.
\v 10 Because¦98493 the¦98480 law¦98482 conquers even affection towards parents¦98486, not¦98489 surrendering virtue¦98492 on their account.
\v 11 It prevails over love for¦98500 one's wife, rebuking her when¦98495 she¦98502 breaks the¦98496 law.
\v 12 It lords it over the love of parents towards their children, for they punish them for vice. It domineers over the intimacy of friends, reproving them when wicked.
\v 13 Don't think it's a strange assertion that reasoning can on behalf of the law conquer even enmity.
\v 14 It doesn't allow cutting down the¦98530 fruit trees of¦98534 an enemy¦98547, but preserves them from the¦98548 destroyers, and¦98551 collects their fallen ruins.
\v 15 Reason appears¦98563 to be master of the¦98561 more violent emotions, like love of empire, empty boasting, and slander.
\v 16 Because¦98574 the temperate understanding repels all¦98573 these malignant emotions, as¦98583 it does anger¦98586; for¦98588 it masters even this.
\v 17 Thus Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add*, when angered against Datan and¦98597 Aviram, did¦98604 nothing to them in anger¦98608, but regulated his anger by reasoning.
\v 18 Because¦98611 the¦98612 temperate mind¦98614 is able¦98610, as I said, to be superior to the emotions, and to correct some and destroy others.
\v 19 Because¦98630 why¦98631 else did our most wise father¦98635 Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add* blame Shim'on \add !(Simeon)\add* and¦98640 Levi for having irrationally slain the¦98645 whole¦98647 race of the Shechemites, saying, “Cursed¦98650 be their anger!”?
\v 20 Because if¦98654 reasoning didn't¦98655 possess the¦98658 power of subduing angry affections, he wouldn't¦98655 have said¦98665 this¦98660.
\v 21 Because¦98668 at the¦98669 time when¦98667 God¦98670 created man¦98672, he¦98676 implanted within him his emotions and¦98677 moral¦98679 nature.
\v 22 At that time he enthroned the mind¦98689 above all¦98685 as the¦98686 holy leader¦98688, through¦98690 the medium of¦98690 the¦98691 senses.
\v 23 He¦98695 gave¦98697 a law¦98696 to¦98697 this mind, by living according¦98698 to¦98698 which¦98699 it'll maintain a temperate, just¦98706, good, and courageous reign¦98701.
\v 24 How¦98711, then¦98712, a man¦98714 may say, if reasoning is master of the emotions, has it no¦98725 control over forgetfulness and¦98723 ignorance¦98724?
\c 3
\p
\v 1 The argument is¦98727 extremely ridiculous, for¦98734 reasoning doesn't appear¦98741 to rule over its own affections, but¦98742 over those of the¦98743 body,
\v 2 in such¦98745 a way as¦98745 that any one¦98747 of you¦98748 may not be¦98750 able¦98750 to root out desire, but¦98752 reasoning will enable you to avoid being enslaved to it.
\v 3 One¦98762 may not be¦98764 able¦98764 to root out anger from the¦98767 soul¦98768, but¦98769 it's possible to¦98773 withstand anger.
\v 4 Any one¦98775 of you¦98776 may not be¦98778 able¦98778 to eradicate malice, but¦98780 reasoning has force to work with you to prevent you yielding to malice¦98785.
\v 5 Because¦98791 reasoning isn't¦98797 an eradicator, but¦98798 an antagonist of the emotions.
\s1 David's example of self-control
\p
\v 6 This may be¦98800 more clearly comprehended from the¦98804 thirst of¦98806 King¦98807 David.
\v 7 Because¦98813 after David had been attacking the¦98817 Pelishtim the¦98819 whole day¦98815, he¦98822 with¦98824 the¦98825 soldiers¦98828 of¦98826 his nation¦98827 killed¦98823 many¦98814 of them;
\v 8 then¦98829 when¦98830 evening¦98832 came, sweating and¦98834 very¦98835 weary, he came to¦98837 the¦98838 royal tent¦98840, around¦98842 which¦98843 the¦98844 entire¦98845 army of our ancestors¦98847 was encamped.
\v 9 Now all¦98854 the¦98856 rest of them were¦98858 at supper;
\v 10 but¦98860 the king¦98861, being very much thirsty, although¦98865 he had¦98867 numerous springs, couldn't¦98870 by their means quench his thirst¦98864;
\v 11 but¦98876 a certain irrational longing for the water¦98885 in the enemy's camp grew stronger and fiercer on him, undid and consumed him.
\v 12 Therefore his bodyguards being troubled at this longing of¦98896 the¦98896 king¦98897, two¦98900 valiant young¦98901 soldiers¦98902, respecting the¦98905 desire of¦98906 the¦98906 king¦98907, fully armed themselves, and¦98912 taking¦98914 a pitcher, got over the¦98916 ramparts of the¦98917 enemies.
\v 13 Unperceived by the¦98922 guardians of the¦98923 gate¦98924, they went throughout the whole¦98929 camp¦98933 of the enemy in quest.
\v 14 Having boldly discovered the¦98937 fountain, they filled¦98941 out¦98939 of it¦98940 the drink for the¦98942 king¦98943.
\v 15 But¦98947 he, though parched with thirst, reasoned that¦98950 a drink regarded of equal value to¦98955 blood¦98963 would be¦98955 terribly dangerous to his soul¦98958.
\v 16 Therefore, setting up reasoning in opposition to his desire¦98967, he poured out the¦98971 drink¦98972 to God¦98974.
\v 17 Because the temperate mind has power to conquer the pressure of the emotions, to quench the fires of excitement,
\v 18 and to wrestle down the pains of the body, however excessive, and through the excellency of reasoning, to spurn all the assaults of the emotions.
\v 19 But¦99012 the¦99014 occasion now invites us to give an illustration of¦99023 temperate reasoning from history.
\v 20 Because at a time when our fathers¦99034 were in possession of undisturbed peace¦99029 through¦99030 obedience to¦99036 the law and¦99037 were prosperous, so¦99040 that¦99042 Seleukos \add !(Seleucus)\add* Nikanor, the¦99043 king¦99045 of Asia, both assigned them money¦99050 for divine service, and¦99056 accepted their form of government,
\v 21 then¦99061 certain people, bringing in new things contrary to¦99064 the¦99065 public harmony, in various ways fell into calamities.
\c 4
\s1 Corruption and conflict in Yerushalem
\p
\v 1 Because¦99083 a certain man named Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add*, who was in opposition to¦99075 an honourable and good¦99085 man who¦99078 once¦99079 held the¦99080 high priesthood for life¦99084, named Honyo \add !(Onias)\add*. After slandering Honyo in every¦99090 way¦99091, Shim'on couldn't injure him with the¦99094 people, so he went away as an¦99099 exile¦99099, with the¦99101 intention of betraying his country¦99095.
\v 2 When coming to¦99106 Apolonios, the¦99108 military governor of Aram \add !(Syria)\add*, Foinikai, and Kilikia, he said,
\v 3 “Having good will to¦99118 the¦99120 king's¦99121 affairs, I've come¦99123 to¦99123 inform you that tens of thousands in private wealth is laid up in¦99129 the¦99130 treasuries of Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* which don't¦99136 belong to the¦99134 temple¦99135, but¦99138 belong to King¦99143 Seleukos.”
\v 4 Apolonios, acquainting himself with the particulars of this, praised Shim'on for his care of the¦99154 king's¦99155 interests, and¦99159 going up to¦99162 Seleukos informed him of the treasure¦99167.
\v 5 Getting¦99169 authority¦99173 about¦99171 it¦99172, and quickly¦99174 advancing into our country with¦99179 the¦99180 accursed Shim'on and¦99183 a very heavy force,
\v 6 he said that¦99187 he came with the¦99188 king's¦99189 commands that¦99193 he should take¦99198 the¦99194 private money¦99199 of¦99196 the¦99196 treasury¦99197.
\v 7 The¦99201 nation¦99202, indignant at this¦99204 proclamation, and¦99208 replying to¦99210 the effect that it was¦99210 extremely unfair that those who'd¦99214 committed deposits to¦99216 the¦99217 sacred treasury¦99219 should be deprived of them, resisted as well as they could.
\v 8 But Apolonios went away with¦99226 threats¦99227 into the¦99233 temple¦99234.
\v 9 The¦99235 priests¦99237, with¦99238 the women¦99239 and¦99240 children¦99241, asked God¦99247 to throw his shield over the¦99249 holy, despised place¦99252,
\v 10 and¦99254 Apolonios was going up with¦99255 his armed force to¦99261 seize the treasure, when angels¦99269 from¦99266 heaven¦99266 appeared riding on horseback, all radiant in armour, filling them with much¦99274 fear¦99276 and trembling.
\v 11 Apolonios fell half dead on the¦99285 courtyard which is open to¦99287 all nations, and extended his hands¦99294 to¦99295 heaven¦99298, and implored the Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*, with¦99299 tears, to pray for him, and take away the anger of the¦99308 heavenly¦99309 army.
\v 12 Because¦99313 he said that he'd sinned, so¦99315 as to¦99317 be consequently worthy¦99318 of death¦99317, and that if he were saved, he'd proclaim to¦99319 all¦99320 people the¦99325 blessedness of¦99326 the¦99326 holy place¦99328.
\v 13 Honyo the¦99332 high priest, induced by these words¦99333, although¦99337 for other reasons anxious that¦99343 King¦99344 Seleukos¦99345 wouldn't suppose that Apolonios was slain by human¦99347 device and¦99349 not¦99350 by Divine¦99351 punishment, prayed for him;
\v 14 and¦99359 he being thus unexpectedly saved, departed to report to the¦99366 king¦99367 what had happened to him.
\v 15 But¦99372 on the death of¦99374 Seleukos the¦99374 king¦99375, his son¦99380 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* Epifanes \add !(Epiphanes)\add* succeeded to the kingdom—a terrible man¦99384 of arrogant¦99385 pride.
\s1 Yason and Antiokos undermine God's law
\p
\v 16 He, having deposed Honyo \add !(Onias)\add* from the high priesthood, appointed his brother Yason to be high priest,
\v 17 who'd made a covenant, if he'd give him this authority, to pay yearly three thousand and six hundred and sixty talents.
\v 18 He¦99416 committed to¦99415 him the high priesthood and¦99418 rulership over the¦99419 nation¦99420.
\v 19 He both changed the manner of living of the people, and¦99427 perverted their civil customs into¦99429 all¦99430 lawlessness.
\v 20 So¦99432 that he not¦99433 only¦99434 erected a gymnasium on¦99435 the very citadel of our country, but neglected the guardianship of¦99445 the¦99445 temple¦99446.
\v 21 Because of that, Divine vengeance was grieved and instigated Antiokos himself against¦99448 them.
\v 22 Because being at war¦99461 with Ptolemaios \add !(Ptolemy)\add* in Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add*, he heard¦99467 that¦99469 on a report of his death being¦99462 spread abroad, the¦99465 inhabitants of Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* had greatly rejoiced, and¦99468 he¦99475 quickly¦99482 marched against¦99483 them.
\v 23 Having subdued them, he¦99489 established a decree¦99490 that¦99492 if¦99493 any of them lived according to the¦99497 ancestral laws¦99500, he should die.
\v 24 When¦99502 he could by no means destroy¦99508 by his decrees¦99511 the¦99510 obedience to the¦99512 law of¦99513 the¦99513 nation¦99514, but¦99516 saw all¦99517 his threats¦99520 and¦99521 punishments¦99522 without effect,
\v 25 for even women¦99527, because¦99528 they continued to circumcise their children¦99531, were flung down a precipice along with¦99532 them, knowing beforehand of the¦99533 punishment.
\v 26 When, therefore¦99541, his decrees¦99543 were disregarded by¦99546 the¦99547 people¦99548, he¦99549 himself compelled by means of¦99550 tortures every one¦99552 of this¦99554 race, by tasting forbidden meats, to renounce the¦99561 Jewish religion.
\c 5
\s1 Antiokos pressures Eleazar to disobey God's law
\p
\v 1 The tyrant Antiokos, therefore, sitting in public state with his assessors on a certain lofty place, with his armed troops standing in a circle around him,
\v 2 commanded his spearbearers to seize every one of the Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*, and to compel them to taste swine's flesh and things offered to idols.
\v 3 Should any of them be unwilling to eat the accursed food, they were to be tortured on the wheel and¦99599 so killed.
\v 4 When¦99608 many had been seized, a foremost man¦99610 of the¦99613 assembly, a Hebrew, by name¦99616 Eleazar, a priest¦99620 by family¦99619, by profession a lawyer¦99623, and¦99624 advanced in years, and¦99628 for this reason known to many of¦99634 the¦99635 king's followers, was brought near¦99639 to him.
\p
\v 5 Antiokos, seeing him, said¦99646,
\v 6 “I'd counsel you, old man, before your tortures begin, to taste the swine's flesh, and save your life; for I feel respect for your age and hoary head, which since you've had so long, you appear to me to be no philosopher in retaining the superstition of the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*.
\v 7 Because therefore, since nature has conferred on you the most excellent flesh of this animal, do you loathe it?
\v 8 It seems senseless not to enjoy what's pleasant, yet not disgraceful; and from notions of sinfulness, to reject the¦99688 gifts of nature¦99689.
\v 9 You'll be acting, I think, still more senselessly, if you follow vain conceits about¦99710 the truth.
\v 10 You will, moreover, be despising me to your own punishment.
\v 11 won't you awake from your trifling philosophy, give up the folly of your notions, and regaining understanding worthy of your age, search into the truth of an expedient course?
\v 12 won't you respect my kindly admonition and¦99761 have pity on your own years?
\v 13 Because bear in mind that¦99774 if¦99775 there's¦99778 any power¦99783 which watches over this¦99780 religion¦99781 of yours, it'll pardon you¦99785 for all¦99787 transgressions of the law which you commit through compulsion.”
\s1 Eleazar refuses to eat forbidden food
\p
\v 14 While the¦99793 tyrant¦99801 incited him in this manner to¦99795 the¦99796 unlawful eating of¦99800 meat, Eleazar begged permission to speak.
\v 15 Having received permission to¦99809 speak¦99809, he began to address the people as follows:
\v 16 “We, Oh Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, who are persuaded that we live under a divine law¦99818, consider no compulsion to¦99823 be¦99823 so forcible as obedience to¦99826 that¦99827 law¦99828.
\v 17 Therefore we consider that we ought not to¦99833 transgress the law in any way¦99835.
\v 18 Indeed, were¦99845 our law¦99847 (as you¦99850 suppose) not truly divine¦99852, and¦99854 if¦99840 we¦99848 wrongly think it¦99856 divine¦99858, we'd¦99862 have no right¦99843 even in that¦99864 case to¦99865 destroy our sense of religion.
\v 19 Don't¦99870 think that¦99875 eating unclean meat is¦99873 a trifling offence.
\v 20 Because transgression of the¦99879 law, whether in small or great matters, is¦99888 of equal importance;
\v 21 for¦99891 in either case the¦99894 law¦99895 is equally slighted.
\v 22 But¦99898 you deride our philosophy, as¦99902 though we¦99899 lived in¦99906 it¦99907 irrationally.
\v 23 Yet it instructs us in self-control, so¦99910 that we're¦99912 superior to all¦99915 pleasures¦99917 and¦99918 lusts; and¦99921 it trains us in courage, so¦99924 that we cheerfully undergo every¦99925 grievance.
\v 24 It instructs¦99931 us in justice, so¦99932 that in all¦99934 our dealings we render what's due. It teaches¦99940 us piety¦99939, so¦99941 that we properly worship the one and only¦99942 God¦99945.
\v 25 That's why we don't eat the unclean; for believing that the¦99955 law¦99956 was established by God¦99953, we're convinced that¦99958 the¦99965 Creator¦99968 of¦99966 the¦99966 world¦99967, in giving his laws, sympathises with our nature¦99961.
\v 26 Those things which are suitable for our souls¦99974, he's directed us to¦99976 eat¦99976; but¦99978 those which aren't, he has forbidden.
\v 27 But¦99983, tyrant-like, you not only¦99985 force us to¦99986 break the law, but¦99989 also¦99990 to¦99991 eat¦99991, that¦99992 you may ridicule us as we¦99995 thus profanely eat.
\v 28 But¦100000 you won't have this cause of laughter against me,
\v 29 nor will I transgress the sacred oaths¦100018 of¦100014 my forefathers to¦100015 keep¦100015 the¦100016 law¦100017.
\v 30 No, not¦100021 if¦100022 you pluck out my eyes¦100026, and¦100027 consume my entrails¦100029.
\v 31 I'm¦100036 not so¦100039 old¦100035, and¦100037 void of¦100041 courage as to not¦100044 be youthful in reason and in defence of my religion.
\v 32 Now then, prepare your wheels, and¦100052 kindle a fiercer flame¦100054.
\v 33 I won't so¦100063 pity¦100059 my old¦100062 age¦100062, as on my account to break the law¦100070 of my country.
\v 34 I won't¦100076 play false to you, Oh law, my instructor, or forsake you, Oh beloved self-control¦100080!
\v 35 I won't¦100081 put you to¦100082 shame, Oh philosopher Reason, or deny you, Oh honoured priesthood¦100089 and¦100091 knowledge¦100093 of the law.
\v 36 Mouth¦100100! You mustn't¦100101 disgrace my old age¦100104, nor the full stature of a perfect life¦100103.
\v 37 My ancestors will receive me as pure¦100105, not¦100110 having feared your compulsion, even to death¦100115.
\v 38 Because¦100119 you'll rule like a tyrant over the¦100121 ungodly¦100117, but¦100122 you won't lord it over my¦100123 thoughts¦100127 about¦100124 religion, either by your arguments, or through¦100132 actions¦100133.”
\c 6
\s1 Eleazar endures torture
\p
\v 1 When Eleazar had in this manner answered the exhortations of¦100139 the¦100139 tyrant¦100140, the¦100143 spearbearers came up, and rudely dragged Eleazar to¦100147 the instruments of torture.
\v 2 First¦100153, they stripped the¦100156 old man¦100154, adorned as he was with the¦100160 beauty of piety¦100161.
\v 3 Then¦100163 tying back his arms and hands, they disdainfully flogged him.
\v 4 A herald opposite cried out, “Obey the¦100170 king's¦100171 commands!”
\p
\v 5 But the¦100176 high-minded¦100178 and¦100179 truly¦100182 noble¦100180 Eleazar, as¦100184 one tortured¦100187 in¦100185 a dream, ignored it.
\v 6 But raising his eyes on high to heaven, the old man's flesh was stripped off by the scourges, and his blood streamed down, and his sides were pierced through.
\v 7 Falling on the ground from his body having no power to endure the pains, he still kept his reasoning upright and unbending.
\v 8 Then one¦100239 of the harsh spearbearers rushed at him and began to kick him in the side to force him to get up again after he fell.
\v 9 But¦100250 he endured the¦100252 pains, despised the¦100256 cruelty, and¦100258 persevered through the¦100260 indignities.
\v 10 Like¦100263 a noble athlete, the old¦100271 man¦100271, when¦100262 struck, vanquished his torturers.
\v 11 His face¦100276 sweating, and he panting for breath, he was admired even by¦100281 the torturers for his courage.
\s1 Eleazar refuses to pretend
\p
\v 12 Therefore, partly in pity for his old age,
\v 13 partly from the sympathy of acquaintance, and partly in admiration of his endurance, some of the attendants of the king said,
\v 14 “Why do you unreasonably destroy yourself, Oh Eleazar, with these miseries?
\v 15 We'll¦100325 bring you¦100331 some meat cooked by yourself, and¦100332 you can save yourself by pretending that¦100334 you've eaten¦100329 swine's flesh.”
\p
\v 16 Eleazar, as¦100341 though the¦100344 advice more painfully tortured him, cried¦100347 out¦100347,
\v 17 “Let us who¦100352 are children of Avraham not be so¦100355 evil advised as by giving way to make use of an unbecoming pretence.
\v 18 Because it'd be irrational, if¦100364 having lived¦100371 up to¦100365 old age in all truth¦100366, and¦100372 having scrupulously guarded¦100378 our character for it¦100375, we'd now¦100379 turn back
\v 19 and¦100381 ourselves become a¦100391 pattern of impiety to¦100392 the¦100393 young, as being an example¦100389 of eating pollution.
\v 20 It'd be disgraceful if we'd live on some short time, and that scorned by all men for cowardice,
\v 21 and be condemned by the tyrant for cowardice by not contending to the death for our divine law.
\v 22 Therefore you¦100427, Oh¦100429 children of Avraham, die nobly for¦100433 your religion.
\v 23 You spearbearers of¦100439 the¦100439 tyrant¦100440, why do you linger?”
\s1 Eleazar's final prayer and death
\p
\v 24 Listening him so high-minded against misery, and¦100451 not changing at their pity, they led him to¦100453 the¦100454 fire¦100459.
\v 25 Then with their wickedly contrived instruments they burnt him on the¦100473 fire, and¦100469 poured stinking fluids down into his nostrils.
\p
\v 26 He being at length burnt down to the bones, and about to expire, raised his eyes Godward, and said,
\v 27 “You know, Oh God, that when I might have been saved, I'm slain for the sake of the law by tortures of fire.
\v 28 Be merciful to your people, and be satisfied with the punishment of me on their account.
\v 29 Let my¦100522 blood¦100523 be a purification for them, and¦100524 take¦100527 my¦100529 life in exchange for theirs.”
\v 30 Thus speaking, the¦100534 holy man¦100536 departed, noble in his torments¦100539, and¦100541 even to the¦100543 agonies of¦100544 death¦100545 resisted in his reasoning for the¦100548 sake of¦100550 the¦100551 law¦100552.
\v 31 Confessedly, therefore, religious reasoning is master¦100555 of the emotions.
\v 32 Because¦100563 had the emotions been superior to reasoning, I'd have given them the witness of¦100566 this mastery.
\v 33 But now¦100576, since reasoning conquered the emotions, we befittingly award it¦100583 the authority¦100589 of first place.
\v 34 It's only fair that we should allow that the power belongs to reasoning, since it masters external miseries.
\v 35 It'd be ridiculous if it weren't so. I prove that reasoning hasn't only mastered pains, but that it's also superior to the pleasures, and withstands them.
\c 7
\s1 Eleazar's example of faith and self-control
\p
\v 1 The¦100631 reasoning of¦100632 our father¦100633 Eleazar, like a first-rate pilot, steering the vessel of piety¦100640 in¦100642 the sea¦100646 of emotions,
\v 2 and¦100647 flouted by the¦100649 threats of¦100650 the¦100650 tyrant¦100651, and¦100653 overwhelmed with the breakers of torture,
\v 3 in no way¦100661 shifted the rudder of piety¦100665 until¦100667 it sailed into¦100670 the harbour¦100675 of victory over death¦100673.
\v 4 No¦100676 besieged city¦100678 has ever held out against many and¦100680 various¦100681 war machines as that holy man did when¦100684 his pious soul¦100692 was tried with the fiery trial of tortures and¦100694 rackings and¦100695 moved his besiegers through¦100701 the¦100702 religious reasoning that shielded him.
\v 5 Because¦100708 father¦100715 Eleazar, projecting his disposition, broke the¦100719 raging waves of the¦100721 emotions as with a jutting cliff.
\v 6 Oh¦100724 priest worthy of the¦100726 priesthood¦100727! You didn't¦100734 pollute your sacred teeth¦100733, nor make your appetite, which had always embraced the¦100735 clean¦100738 and¦100737 lawful, a partaker of profanity.
\v 7 Oh¦100743 harmoniser with the law¦100745, and¦100746 sage devoted to a divine life¦100749!
\v 8 Of such¦100750 a character ought those to¦100752 be¦100752 who perform the duties of the¦100755 law¦100756 at the risk of their own¦100757 blood¦100758, and¦100759 defend it with generous sweat by sufferings even to death¦100764.
\v 9 You¦100767, father¦100768, have gloriously established our right government by your endurance; and making of¦100772 much account our past service, prevented its destruction, and¦100778 by your actions, have made credible the¦100779 words¦100792 of¦100785 philosophy¦100791.
\v 10 Oh¦100793 aged man¦100796 of more power than tortures, elder more vigorous than fire¦100797, greatest king¦100803 over the emotions, Eleazar¦100804!
\v 11 Because as¦100805 father¦100808 Aharon \add !(Aaron)\add*, armed with a censer, hastening through¦100813 the¦100814 consuming fire, vanquished the¦100817 flame-bearing angel¦100820,
\v 12 so, Eleazar, the¦100822 descendant of Aharon, wasted away by the¦100826 fire¦100827, didn't give up his reasoning.
\v 13 What's most wonderful is that though he was an¦100836 old¦100836 man¦100836, though the¦100841 labours of¦100842 his body¦100843 were now spent, his muscles were relaxed, and his sinews worn out, he recovered youth.
\v 14 By the¦100856 spirit¦100857 of¦100858 reasoning, and¦100860 the¦100861 reasoning of Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add*, he rendered powerless the¦100864 many-headed rack.
\v 15 Oh¦100868 blessed¦100869 old age, and¦100871 reverend hoar head, and¦100874 life¦100875 obedient to the law, which¦100877 the faithful¦100878 seal¦100880 of death¦100879 perfected¦100881.
\v 16 If¦100882, then¦100884, an¦100885 old¦100885 man¦100885, through¦100891 religion, despised tortures even to¦100895 death¦100888, then certainly religious reasoning is¦100895 ruler¦100894 of the emotions.
\v 17 But perhaps¦100901 some might say, “It isn't all¦100909 who¦100911 conquer emotions, as not¦100912 all¦100913 possess wise reasoning.”
\v 18 But¦100918 those who've meditated on religion with their whole¦100923 heart¦100924, these alone¦100926 can master the¦100929 emotions of the¦100930 flesh¦100931:
\v 19 they who¦100933 believe that¦100935 to God¦100936 they don't die¦100938; for¦100940, as¦100939 our forefathers, Avraham, Yitshak, and Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add*, they live¦100947 to God¦100949.
\v 20 This circumstance, then¦100951, is by no means an objection, that¦100953 some who¦100958 have weak¦100959 reasoning are governed by their emotions,
\v 21 since¦100961 what person, walking religiously by the whole¦100964 rule¦100968 of philosophy¦100967, and¦100971 believing in¦100972 God¦100973,
\v 22 and¦100974 knowing that¦100976 it's a blessed¦100983 thing to endure all¦100980 kinds of hardships for virtue¦100979, wouldn't, for the¦100988 sake of¦100990 religion, master his emotion?
\v 23 Because¦100994 only the¦100995 wise¦100996 and¦100997 brave man is¦101000 lord¦101003 over his emotions.
\v 24 This is why even boys, trained with the philosophy of¦101004 religious reasoning, have conquered still more bitter tortures;
\v 25 for¦101019 when the¦101021 tyrant¦101027 was manifestly vanquished in his first¦101022 attempt, in being unable to force the¦101026 old man¦101048 to eat the¦101033 unclean thing,
\c 8
\nb
\s1 Seven brothers face the king's threats
\p
\v 1 then¦101034, indeed, vehemently swayed with emotion, he commanded to¦101038 bring¦101045 others of the¦101041 adult Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*, and if they'd eat of the¦101043 unclean thing, to¦101045 let them go when they'd eaten; but if¦101047 they objected, to¦101050 torment them more grievously.
\v 2 The¦101059 tyrant¦101060 having given this¦101057 charge, seven¦101066 kindred were brought into his presence, along with¦101063 their aged mother¦101065. They were handsome, modest, well-born, and altogether comely.
\v 3 When the¦101080 tyrant¦101081 saw them encircling their mother¦101088 as¦101082 in¦101083 a dance, he¦101091 was pleased with them. Being struck with their becoming and innocent manner, smiled at them, and calling¦101103 them near¦101102, said¦101104,
\v 4 “O youths, with favourable feelings, I admire the beauty of each of you. Greatly honouring so numerous a band of kindred, I not only counsel you not to share the madness of the old man who's been tortured before,
\v 5 but I beg you to yield, and to enjoy my friendship; for I possess the power, not only of punishing those who disobey my commands, but of doing good to those who obey them.
\v 6 Put confidence in me, then¦101154, and¦101155 you'll receive¦101162 places of authority in my¦101159 government, if you forsake your national way of life,
\v 7 and¦101170, conforming to the Greek way of life¦101173, alter your rule and¦101174 revel in youth's delights.
\v 8 Because¦101180 if¦101181 you provoke me by your disobedience¦101187, you'll¦101188 compel me to¦101191 destroy¦101200 every one of you with terrible punishments by tortures.
\v 9 Have mercy, then¦101202, on your¦101203 own selves, whom I, although an enemy, am compassionate for your age¦101211 and¦101212 attractive appearance.
\v 10 won't you consider this¦101218: that¦101219 if you¦101221 disobey¦101222, there'll be nothing left for you but¦101223 to¦101227 die¦101227 in torture?”
\p
\v 11 When he'd¦101229 said this¦101234, he ordered¦101232 the¦101237 instruments of torture to be brought forward, that¦101239 fear¦101243 might prevail on them to eat unclean meat.
\v 12 When the spearman brought forward the wheels, the racks, the hooks, racks, cauldrons, pans, finger-racks, iron hands and wedges, and bellows, the tyrant continued:
\v 13 “Fear, young men, and the righteousness which you worship will be merciful to you if you transgress because of compulsion.”
\p
\v 14 Now they having listened to¦101296 these words of persuasion, and¦101298 seeing¦101299 the fearful instruments, not only¦101302 weren't afraid¦101304, but¦101305 even answered the arguments of the¦101308 tyrant¦101309, and¦101310 through¦101311 their good reasoning destroyed his power.
\v 15 Now let's consider the matter. Had any of them been¦101324 weak-spirited and¦101325 cowardly among¦101327 them, what reasoning would they've employed but these?
\v 16 “O wretched that we¦101337 are, and¦101338 extremely senseless! When the king¦101341 exhorts us, and¦101344 calls us to¦101345 his bounty, shouldn't¦101348 we obey him?
\v 17 Why do we cheer ourselves with vain counsels, and¦101356 venture on a disobedience¦101358 bringing death?
\v 18 Shouldn't we fear, Oh kindred, the¦101364 instruments of torture and¦101366 weigh the threatenings of torment and¦101372 shun this vain-glory and¦101377 destructive pride?
\v 19 Let's have compassion on our age¦101383 and¦101384 relent over the years of our mother¦101388.
\v 20 Let's bear in mind that¦101392 we'll be dying as rebels.
\v 21 Divine Justice will pardon us if we¦101397 fear the¦101399 king¦101405 through¦101402 necessity¦101403.
\v 22 Why withdraw ourselves from a most sweet life¦101412, and¦101413 deprive ourselves of¦101416 this pleasant world¦101418?
\v 23 Let's not¦101419 oppose necessity¦101422, nor¦101423 seek vain-glory by our own torture¦101428.
\v 24 The¦101431 law itself wouldn't arbitrarily put us to death because we¦101434 dread torture.
\v 25 Why has such angry zeal taken root in us, and¦101445 such fatal obstinacy approved itself to us, when we¦101440 might live unmolested by the¦101454 king¦101455?”
\p
\v 26 But¦101457 the young men didn't¦101465 say¦101460 or think anything of this¦101461 kind when¦101465 about to¦101464 be tortured.
\v 27 Because they were well aware of the sufferings, and masters of the pains.
\v 28-29 So that as soon as the tyrant had ceased counselling them to eat the unclean, they all with one voice, as from the same heart said,
\c 9
\nb
\v 1 “Why do you delay, Oh¦101498 tyrant¦101499? Because¦101501 we're¦101502 more ready¦101500 to¦101503 die¦101503 than¦101504 to transgress the¦101506 injunctions of our fathers.
\v 2 We'd be disgracing our fathers if¦101516 we didn't¦101517 obey the¦101519 law¦101520, and¦101522 take knowledge¦101524 for our guide.
\v 3 Oh tyrant¦101527, counsellor of law-breaking, don't¦101529, hating¦101531 us as you do, pity us more than we¦101534 pity ourselves.
\v 4 Because¦101537 we consider your escape to¦101542 be¦101542 worse than death¦101540.
\v 5 You try to scare us by threatening us with death¦101559 by tortures, as¦101561 though you'd learnt¦101567 nothing by the death of Eleazar.
\v 6 But¦101569 if aged men of the¦101570 Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add* have died in the¦101572 cause of¦101574 religion after enduring torture, more rightly should we younger men die¦101580, scorning your cruel tortures, which¦101593 our aged instructor¦101596 overcame.
\v 7 Make the attempt, then¦101602, Oh tyrant¦101601. If¦101606 you put us to death for¦101608 our religion, don't¦101611 think that¦101609 you harm us by torturing us.
\v 8 Because we¦101616 through¦101619 this¦101621 ill-treatment and¦101623 endurance will gain the rewards of virtue.
\v 9 But you¦101630, for the wicked and¦101631 despotic slaughter of¦101632 us, will, from the¦101639 Divine¦101640 vengeance, endure eternal¦101642 torture by fire¦101645.”
\s1 The first two brothers are killed
\p
\v 10 When¦101659 they'd said this¦101646, the¦101655 tyrant¦101656 wasn't only¦101650 exasperated against them for being disobedient¦101653, but¦101657 enraged with them for being ungrateful.
\v 11 So, at his bidding, the¦101664 torturers brought the¦101669 oldest of them, and¦101671 tearing¦101672 through his tunic¦101674, bound his hands¦101677 and¦101679 arms on each side with straps¦101682.
\v 12 When¦101685 they'd laboured hard without effect in scourging him, they hurled him on¦101694 the¦101695 wheel¦101696.
\v 13 The¦101700 noble¦101701 youth, extended on this, became dislocated.
\v 14 With every¦101707 member¦101708 disjointed, he denounced the tyrant, saying,
\v 15 “O most accursed tyrant¦101712, and¦101714 enemy¦101718 of heavenly¦101716 justice, and¦101719 cruel-hearted, I'm no¦101721 murderer, nor sacrilegious man, whom¦101726 you torture, but¦101730 a defender of the Divine law¦101732.”
\p
\v 16 And¦101734 when the¦101735 spearmen said, “Consent to¦101739 eat¦101739, that¦101742 you may be released from your tortures,”
\v 17 he answered, “Not so¦101757 powerful, Oh accursed lackeys, is your wheel, as to stifle my reasoning. Cut my limbs, and¦101765 burn my flesh¦101768, and¦101769 twist my joints.
\v 18 Because through all¦101774 my torments I'll convince you¦101776 that¦101780 the children of the Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add* are alone¦101781 unconquered on behalf of virtue.”
\p
\v 19 While he was saying this, they heaped up fuel, and setting fire¦101791 to it, strained him on the¦101795 wheel¦101796 still more.
\v 20 The¦101802 wheel¦101803 was defiled all¦101800 over with blood¦101801. The hot ashes were quenched by the droppings of gore, and¦101814 pieces of flesh were scattered about¦101815 the¦101816 axles of¦101818 the machine.
\v 21 Although the framework of¦101830 his bones¦101829 was now destroyed, the¦101831 high-minded¦101832 and¦101833 Abrahamic youth didn't groan¦101837.
\v 22 But¦101838, as¦101839 though transformed by fire¦101841 into¦101845 immortality, he nobly endured¦101845 the¦101847 rackings, saying,
\v 23 “Imitate me, Oh kindred. Never desert your station, nor renounce my brotherhood in courage. Fight the holy and honourable fight of religion,
\v 24 by which means our just and paternal Providence, becoming merciful to the nation, will punish the pestilent tyrant.”
\v 25 Saying¦101891 this¦101892, the¦101896 revered youth abruptly closed his life.
\p
\v 26 When¦101899 all¦101900 admired his courageous soul¦101903, the¦101905 spearmen brought forward him who was second¦101912 oldest, and¦101913 having put on iron gauntlets with sharp hooks, bound him to the rack.
\v 27 When¦101926, on enquiring whether he'd eat¦101928 before¦101930 he was tortured, they heard¦101936 his noble sentiment.
\v 28 After they with the iron gauntlets had violently dragged all¦101950 the¦101948 flesh¦101949 from the neck to the chin, the panther-like animals tore off the very skin of his head¦101954, but¦101961 he¦101962, bearing with firmness this¦101964 misery, said,
\v 29 “How¦101968 sweet is every¦101970 form of death¦101972 for¦101973 the religion of our fathers!” Then¦101979 he said¦101978 to¦101980 the¦101981 tyrant¦101982,
\v 30 “Don't you think¦101984, most cruel of all¦101985 tyrants¦101987, that you're¦101990 now¦101991 tortured more than I, finding your arrogant conception of tyranny conquered by¦102001 our perseverance¦102007 in behalf of our religion?
\v 31 Because¦102012 I lighten my suffering by the¦102013 pleasures¦102015 which are connected with virtue¦102014.
\v 32 But you're tortured with threatenings for impiety. You¦102019 won't escape¦102028, most corrupt tyrant¦102031, the vengeance of Divine¦102034 anger¦102035.”
\c 10
\s1 The third and fourth brothers remain faithful
\p
\v 1 Now this¦102038 one endured this¦102039 praiseworthy death¦102041. The¦102043 third¦102044 was brought¦102045 along, and exhorted by¦102048 many to taste and save his life.
\v 2 But he cried out and said¦102056, “Don't you know that¦102059 the father of those who are dead is my father also, and¦102066 that the¦102067 same mother¦102069 bore me, and¦102071 that I was brought up in the¦102073 same way?
\v 3 I don't renounce the noble relationship of my kindred.
\v 4 Now then, whatever instrument of vengeance you have, apply it to my body, for you aren't able to touch my soul, even if you want to.”
\v 5 But¦102104 they, highly incensed at his boldness¦102108 of¦102109 speech, dislocated his hands¦102114 and¦102116 feet¦102118 with racking engines, and¦102120 wrenching them from¦102121 their sockets, dismembered him.
\v 6 They dragged around his fingers¦102127, his arms, his legs¦102133, and¦102134 his ankles.
\v 7 Not being able by any means to strangle him, they tore off his skin, together with the extreme tips of his fingers, and then dragged him to the wheel,
\v 8 around which his vertebral joints were loosened, and he saw his own flesh torn to shreds, and streams of blood flowing from his entrails.
\v 9 When¦102178 about to¦102179 die¦102179, he said,
\v 10 “We¦102181, Oh¦102183 accursed tyrant¦102185, suffer¦102192 this¦102191 for¦102186 the sake of¦102186 Divine education and¦102188 virtue¦102189.
\v 11 But you¦102193, for¦102195 your impiety and¦102198 blood shedding, will endure unceasing torments.”
\p
\v 12 Thus having died worthily of his kindred, they dragged forward the¦102207 fourth¦102208, saying,
\v 13 “Don't¦102211 share the madness of your¦102219 kindred, but¦102221 respect the¦102223 king¦102224 and save yourself¦102226.”
\p
\v 14 But¦102228 he¦102229 said¦102230 to¦102234 them, “You don't have¦102234 a fire¦102238 so¦102239 scorching as to make me play the coward.
\v 15 By¦102242 the¦102243 blessed¦102244 death¦102248 of my kindred, and¦102249 the¦102250 eternal¦102251 punishment of¦102252 the¦102252 tyrant¦102253, and¦102255 the glorious life¦102260 of the pious, I won't repudiate the noble brotherhood¦102265.
\v 16 Invent, Oh tyrant¦102267, tortures, that¦102269 you may learn, even through¦102271 them, that¦102274 I'm¦102276 the¦102277 brother¦102275 of those tormented before.”
\p
\v 17 When he'd said this¦102279, the¦102281 blood-thirsty, murderous, and unholy Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* ordered¦102288 his tongue to¦102288 be cut out.
\v 18 But¦102294 he said¦102295, “Even¦102296 if¦102296 you take away the organ of speech, God¦102306 still hears¦102304 the¦102305 silent.
\v 19 Listen, my tongue¦102310 is extended, cut it¦102315 off; for¦102313 in spite of that¦102316 you won't silence our reasoning.
\v 20 We gladly¦102320 lose our limbs on behalf of¦102322 God¦102323.
\v 21 But¦102330 God¦102334 will speedily find you, since you cut off the¦102335 tongue, the¦102337 instrument of divine melody.”
\c 11
\s1 The fifth and sixth brothers face death
\p
\v 1 When he'd¦102346 died, disfigured in his torments¦102348, the¦102351 fifth¦102352 leapt forward, and said,
\v 2 “I don't intend, Oh tyrant¦102357, to¦102358 get excused¦102364 from the torment¦102363 which is on behalf of virtue.
\v 3 But¦102366 I've¦102371 come of my own accord, that¦102370 by my death you may owe heavenly¦102378 vengeance and punishment for more crimes.
\v 4 Oh¦102381 you hater of virtue and¦102383 of men, what have we¦102387 done that¦102390 you thus revel in our blood?
\v 5 Does it seem evil¦102393 to you¦102394 that¦102396 we worship the¦102397 Founder of all¦102398 things, and¦102401 live¦102406 according¦102402 to¦102402 his surpassing law¦102407?
\v 6 But this is worthy of honours, not torments,
\v 7 if you'd been capable of the higher feelings of men, and possessed the hope of salvation from God.
\v 8 Listen now, being alien from God, you make war against those who are religious towards God.”
\p
\v 9 As¦102436 he said this¦102436, the¦102438 spearbearers bound¦102440 him and drew¦102442 him to¦102443 the¦102444 rack,
\v 10 to¦102446 which¦102447 binding¦102448 him at his knees¦102452, and¦102453 fastening them with iron fetters, they bent down his loins¦102459 on¦102461 the¦102462 wedge of the¦102470 wheel; and his body was then dismembered, scorpion-fashion.
\v 11 With his breath thus confined, and his body strangled, he said,
\v 12 “A great favour you bestow on us, Oh tyrant, by enabling us to manifest our adherence to the law by means of nobler sufferings.”
\p
\v 13 He¦102510 also being dead, the¦102511 sixth¦102512, quite a youth, was brought¦102513 out. On the¦102517 tyrant¦102518 asking him whether he'd eat and¦102524 be delivered, he said¦102525,
\v 14 “I'm¦102526 indeed younger than my brothers¦102531, but in understanding I'm¦102533 as old;
\v 15 for¦102542 having been born and reared to the¦102548 same end. We're bound to¦102551 die¦102551 also on behalf of the same cause.
\v 16 So¦102554 that if¦102555 you¦102556 think¦102557 it's proper to torment us for not¦102559 eating the unclean, then torment!”
\p
\v 17 As he said¦102564 this, they brought him to¦102566 the¦102567 wheel¦102568.
\v 18 Extended on¦102569 this, with limbs racked and¦102573 dislocated, he was gradually roasted from beneath.
\v 19 Having heated sharp¦102578 spits, they approached them to¦102582 his back; and¦102583 having transfixed his sides, they burnt away¦102587 his entrails¦102589.
\v 20 He, while tormented, said, “O good and¦102592 holy contest, in which¦102599 for¦102600 the sake of¦102600 religion, we kindred have been called to the¦102601 arena of pain, and haven't been conquered.
\v 21 Because¦102612 religious understanding, Oh¦102614 tyrant¦102615, is unconquered.
\v 22 Armed¦102620 with upright virtue, I¦102622 also will depart with¦102623 my¦102626 kindred.
\v 23 I, too, bearing with me a great¦102627 avenger, Oh inventor of tortures, and enemy of the¦102638 truly¦102639 pious.
\v 24 We six¦102641 youths have destroyed your tyranny.
\v 25 Because¦102648 isn't¦102649 your inability to overrule our reasoning, and to compel us to eat the unclean, your destruction?
\v 26 Your fire¦102666 is cold to us. Your racks are painless, and your violence harmless.
\v 27 Because¦102680 the guards not of a tyrant¦102681 but¦102682 of a divine law¦102684 are our defenders. Through¦102689 this we keep our reasoning unconquered.”
\c 12
\s1 The youngest brother refuses to give in
\p
\v 1 When¦102697 he, too, had undergone blessed martyrdom, and¦102698 died in the cauldron into which¦102696 he'd¦102699 been thrown, the¦102705 seventh¦102706, the youngest of all¦102708, came forward,
\v 2 whom the¦102712 tyrant¦102713 pitying, though¦102714 he'd been dreadfully reproached by¦102716 his kindred,
\v 3 seeing him already encompassed with chains, had him brought nearer, and endeavoured to counsel him, saying,
\v 4 “You see the end of the madness of your kindred, for they've died in torture through disobedience. You, if disobedient, having been miserably tormented, will yourself perish prematurely.
\v 5 But¦102762 if you obey, you'll be my friend¦102763, and¦102765 have a charge over the¦102766 affairs of the¦102768 kingdom¦102769.”
\v 6 Having thus exhorted him, he¦102773 sent for the¦102775 boy's mother¦102776, that¦102780, by showing compassion to her for the loss of so¦102772 many sons¦102783, he might incline her, through the hope of safety, to render the survivor obedient.
\p
\v 7 He, after his mother had urged him on in the Hebrew tongue, (as we'll soon relate) said,
\v 8 “Release me that I may speak to the king and all his friends.”
\v 9 They, rejoicing greatly at the¦102822 promise¦102823 of¦102824 the¦102824 youth, quickly¦102826 let him go.
\v 10 He, running up to¦102831 the¦102833 pans, said¦102835,
\v 11 “Impious tyrant¦102843, and most blasphemous man, were you not ashamed, having received prosperity and a kingdom¦102854 from¦102846 God¦102848, to¦102849 kill His servants, and to rack the doers of godliness?
\v 12 Therefore the divine vengeance is reserving you¦102867 for eternal fire and torments¦102876, which¦102877 will cling to you for all¦102879 time.
\v 13 Weren't you ashamed, man¦102887 as you are, yet most savage, to¦102888 cut out¦102893 the¦102890 tongues of men of like¦102891 feeling and¦102892 origin, and¦102899 having thus abused to¦102904 torture¦102904 them?
\v 14 But¦102905 they, bravely dying¦102909, fulfilled¦102910 their religion towards God¦102914.
\v 15 But you'll groan as you¦102916 deserve for having slain without cause the champions of virtue¦102923.
\v 16 Therefore,” he¦102929 continued, “I myself, being about to¦102930 die¦102930,
\v 17 won't forsake my kindred.
\v 18 I call¦102940 on¦102940 the¦102942 god¦102944 of my fathers to be merciful to my race.
\v 19 But you¦102949, both living and dead, he'll punish.”
\v 20 Thus having prayed, he¦102960 hurled himself¦102962 into¦102964 the¦102965 pans; and¦102967 so expired.
\c 13
\s1 The brothers' faith overcomes suffering
\p
\v 1 If¦102970 then¦102972, the¦102978 seven¦102979 kindred despised troubles even to death¦102975, it's admitted on all¦102982 sides¦102982 that¦102983 righteous reasoning is absolute¦102984 master¦102984 over the emotions.
\v 2 Because just¦102991 as¦102991 if¦102993 they'd eaten of the unholy as slaves to the emotions, we'd have said that¦103001 they'd been conquered by them.
\v 3 Now¦103003 it isn't so¦103004. But¦103007 by means of the reasoning which is praised by God, they mastered their emotions.
\v 4 It's¦103017 impossible to¦103017 overlook the¦103019 leadership of reflection, for¦103022 it gained the victory over both emotions and troubles.
\v 5 How¦103027, then¦103028, can we avoid according to¦103030 these men mastery of emotion through¦103039 right reasoning, since they didn't withdraw from the pains of¦103039 fire¦103040?
\v 6 Because just¦103044 as¦103044 by means of towers projecting in front of harbours men break the¦103049 threatening waves¦103050, and thus assure a still course to¦103054 vessels entering port,
\v 7 so that¦103060 seven-towered¦103061 right-reasoning of the young men, securing the harbour¦103069 of religion, conquered¦103073 the tempest of emotions.
\v 8 Because¦103076 having arranged a holy choir of piety¦103077, they encouraged one¦103081 another¦103081, saying,
\v 9 “Brothers¦103085, may we die brotherly for the¦103087 law¦103088. Let us imitate the¦103090 three¦103091 young men in Ashur \add !(Assyria)\add* who despised the equally afflicting furnace¦103099.
\v 10 Let's not¦103101 be cowards in the manifestation of piety¦103106.”
\v 11 One¦103110 said, “Courage, brother¦103112!” and¦103115 another, “Nobly endure!”
\v 12 Another said, “Remember¦103121 of what stock you are;” and by the hand¦103127 of¦103129 our father¦103126 Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add* endured¦103132 to be slain for the sake of piety¦103131.
\v 13 One¦103135 and¦103136 all, looking at each¦103137 other serene and confident, said, “Let's sacrifice with all our heart¦103155 our souls¦103159 to¦103148 God¦103150 who gave¦103157 them, and¦103160 employ our bodies¦103168 for the¦103162 keeping of the¦103164 law¦103165.
\v 14 Let's not¦103169 fear¦103170 him who¦103171 thinks¦103172 he kills;
\v 15 for great¦103174 is the trial of soul and¦103178 danger¦103179 of eternal torment laid¦103183 up for those who¦103184 transgress God's¦103189 commandment¦103187.
\v 16 Let's arm ourselves, therefore¦103191, in the self-control, which is divine reasoning.
\v 17 If we suffer¦103198 like this, Avraham, Yitshak, and¦103201 Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add* will receive us, and¦103204 all¦103205 the¦103206 fathers¦103207 will commend us.
\v 18 As each¦103211 one of the¦103212 kindred was hauled away, the¦103217 rest exclaimed, “Don't¦103219 disgrace us, Oh brother, nor falsify those who¦103227 died before you!”
\p
\v 19 Now you aren't¦103230 ignorant¦103230 of the charm of brotherhood, which the Divine and all wise Providence has imparted through fathers to children, and has engendered through the¦103243 mother's womb.
\v 20 In¦103247 which¦103248 these brothers¦103251 having remained an equal¦103250 time¦103253, and¦103254 having been formed for the¦103256 same period¦103258, and¦103260 been increased by the¦103262 same blood¦103264, and¦103266 having been perfected through¦103267 the same principle of life,
\v 21 and¦103272 having been brought forth at equal intervals, and¦103278 having sucked milk from the same springs, hence their brotherly souls¦103290 are reared up lovingly together,
\v 22 and¦103291 increase the more powerfully because¦103294 of¦103294 this simultaneous rearing, and by daily companionship, and by other education, and exercise in¦103308 God's¦103310 law¦103309.
\p
\v 23 Brotherly¦103317 love¦103317 being thus sympathetically constituted, the¦103319 seven¦103320 kindred had¦103323 a more sympathetic mutual harmony.
\v 24 Because¦103329 being educated in the¦103330 same law¦103328, and¦103333 practising the¦103334 same virtues¦103337, and¦103338 reared up in a just¦103340 course of life, they increased this harmony with each other.
\v 25 Because the¦103347 same ardour for¦103348 what's right and honourable increased their goodwill and harmony towards each other.
\v 26 Because¦103359 it¦103363 acting along with religion, made their brotherly¦103366 feeling more desirable to them.
\v 27 And yet, although¦103369 nature¦103371, companionship, and virtuous morals increased their brotherly love, those who were left endured to¦103396 see¦103396 their kindred, who were mistreated for¦103387 their religion, tortured¦103399 even to death¦103398.
\c 14
\p
\v 1 More that this, they even urged them on to¦103402 this¦103403 mistreatment; so that they not¦103407 only¦103408 despised pains themselves, but¦103413 they even got the better of their affections of brotherly¦103418 love¦103418.
\v 2 Reasoning is more royal than a king¦103422, and¦103425 freer than freemen!
\v 3 What a sacred and¦103429 harmonious concert of the¦103434 seven¦103435 kindred as about¦103431 piety¦103433!
\v 4 None¦103438 of the¦103440 seven¦103441 youths turned cowardly or shrank back from death¦103447.
\v 5 But¦103449 all¦103450 of them, as¦103451 though running¦103455 the road¦103454 to¦103455 immortality, hastened on to¦103456 death¦103461 through¦103458 tortures.
\v 6 Because just¦103463 as¦103463 hands¦103465 and¦103466 feet¦103467 are moved sympathetically with the directions of the soul, so those holy youths agreed to¦103485 death¦103490 for religion's sake, as through¦103480 the immortal soul of religion.
\v 7 Oh¦103491 holy seven of harmonious kindred! Because as¦103497 the seven days¦103502 of creation, about¦103503 religion,
\v 8 so the youths, circling around the number seven, annulled the fear¦103517 of torments.
\v 9 We now¦103519 shudder at the recital of the affliction of those young men; but¦103529 they not only¦103531 saw¦103532, and¦103534 not¦103534 only¦103535 heard¦103536 the immediate execution of the threat¦103539, but¦103541 undergoing it, persevered; and that¦103526 through¦103548 the pains of¦103548 fire¦103549.
\v 10 What could be¦103553 more painful? Because¦103556 the¦103559 power¦103563 of¦103560 fire¦103561, being¦103562 sharp¦103555 and¦103557 quick¦103564, speedily dissolved their bodies¦103567.
\s1 A mother's love and faith
\p
\v 11 Don't¦103569 think¦103574 it wonderful that¦103578 reasoning ruled over those men¦103577 in¦103579 their torments¦103581, when¦103584 even a woman's¦103585 mind¦103586 despised more manifold pains.
\v 12 Because the¦103590 mother¦103591 of those seven¦103594 youths endured the¦103597 rackings of each¦103600 of her children¦103602.
\p
\v 13 Consider¦103604 how¦103606 comprehensive is¦103608 the love of offspring, which draws every¦103614 one to¦103615 sympathy of affection,
\v 14 where irrational animals possess a¦103638 similar sympathy and¦103622 love for their offspring with¦103628 men.
\v 15 The¦103641 tame birds¦103642 frequenting the roofs of our houses defend their fledglings.
\v 16 Others build their nests, and¦103654 hatch their young, on the¦103656 tops of mountains¦103658 and in the precipices of valleys, and the holes and tops of trees¦103663, and keep away the intruder.
\v 17 If¦103675 not¦103678 able¦103679 to¦103680 do this, they fly circling round them in agony of affection, calling out in their own¦103689 note, and save their offspring in whatever manner they're¦103693 able¦103693.
\v 18 But¦103698 why should we point attention to¦103708 the¦103709 sympathy towards children¦103710 shown by irrational animals?
\v 19 Even bees, at the season¦103720 of honey-making, attack all who¦103722 approach, and¦103724 pierce with their sting, as¦103725 with a sword, those who draw near their hive, and¦103735 repel them even to death¦103738.
\v 20 But¦103739 sympathy with her children¦103751 didn't¦103740 turn away the mother¦103746 of the¦103749 young men, who had a spirit kindred with that of Avraham.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Oh¦103752 reasoning of the sons, lord over the emotions, and¦103757 religion more desirable to a mother¦103759 than children¦103760!
\v 2 The mother¦103762, when two things were set before her, religion and¦103766 the¦103767 safety of her seven¦103768 sons¦103769 for a time, on the¦103773 conditional promise of¦103774 a tyrant¦103775,
\v 3 rather elected the religion which according¦103786 to¦103786 God¦103787 preserves to eternal¦103784 life¦103785.
\v 4 In what way¦103790 can I describe ethically the affections of parents¦103793 towards¦103800 their children, the resemblance of soul¦103795 and of form impressed into the small¦103801 type of a child¦103802 in a wonderful manner, especially¦103806 through¦103807 the greater sympathy of mothers¦103814 with the feelings of those born of them!
\v 5 Because by how¦103817 much¦103817 mothers are by nature weak in disposition and prolific in offspring, by so¦103825 much the fonder they're¦103827 of children.
\v 6 Of all¦103829 mothers, the mother¦103837 of the¦103839 seven¦103841 was the fondest of children, who in seven childbirths had deeply engendered love towards them.
\v 7 Through¦103849 her many pains undergone in connection with each¦103853 one, she was compelled to¦103860 feel sympathy with them;
\v 8 yet, through¦103862 fear¦103867 of¦103862 God¦103866, she neglected the temporary¦103872 salvation¦103873 of her children¦103871.
\v 9 Not only so¦103876, but¦103877 on account of¦103879 the excellent disposition to¦103886 the¦103887 law¦103888, her maternal affection towards them was increased.
\v 10 Because¦103899 they were¦103900 both just and temperate, and courageous, high-minded, fond of their kindred, and so¦103920 fond of their mother that even to death¦103923 they obeyed her by observing the¦103924 law.
\p
\v 11 Yet, though there were so¦103933 many circumstances connected with love of children to¦103934 draw on a mother¦103942 to¦103943 sympathy, in the case of none¦103944 of them were the various tortures able to pervert her principle.
\v 12 But¦103953 she inclined each one¦103956 separately and¦103958 all¦103960 together¦103959 to¦103963 death¦103968 for religion.
\v 13 Oh¦103969 holy nature¦103970 and parental feeling, and reward of bringing up children, and unconquerable maternal affection!
\v 14 At the racking and roasting of each one¦103985 of¦103993 them, the¦103994 observant mother was prevented by religion from changing.
\v 15 She saw her children's¦103999 flesh¦103997 dissolving around¦104001 the¦104002 fire¦104003, and¦104005 their extremities quivering on the ground¦104013, and¦104015 the flesh¦104024 of their heads¦104018 dropped forward down to their beards, like¦104025 masks.
\p
\v 16 Oh¦104028 you mother¦104032, who was tried at this time with bitterer pangs than¦104035 those at birth!
\v 17 Oh¦104040 you only woman¦104042 who've¦104043 produced perfect holiness!
\v 18 Your firstborn¦104050, expiring, didn't¦104052 turn you, nor the second¦104053, looking miserable in¦104057 his torments¦104058, nor the third¦104060, breathing out his soul.
\v 19 You didn't¦104062 weep when you saw each¦104066 of their eyes¦104064 looking sternly at their tortures, and¦104076 their nostrils foreboding death¦104082!
\v 20 When you saw children's¦104087 flesh¦104089 heaped on children's¦104090 flesh that'd been torn off, heads decapitated on heads, dead¦104099 falling on the dead¦104100, and¦104102 a¦104103 choir of children¦104106 turned through¦104108 torture into a burying ground, you didn't lament.
\v 21 Not¦104117 so¦104117 do siren melodies or songs of swans attract the¦104122 hearers to¦104123 listening¦104123, Oh¦104125 voices of children¦104126 calling on your mother¦104130 in the middle of torments!
\v 22 With what and¦104133 what manner of torments¦104146 was the¦104136 mother¦104137 herself tortured, as her sons¦104139 were undergoing the¦104138 wheel and the fires!
\v 23 But¦104147 religious reasoning, having strengthened her courage in¦104154 the¦104156 middle of sufferings, enabled her to forego, for the¦104160 time, parental love.
\p
\v 24 Although¦104164 seeing the destruction¦104168 of seven¦104165 children¦104166, the¦104170 noble mother¦104172, after one embrace, stripped off her feelings through¦104174 faith¦104178 in God¦104177.
\v 25 Because just¦104179 as¦104179 in a council room, seeing in her own soul¦104185 vehement counsellors, nature¦104189 and¦104190 parentage and¦104192 love of her children, and¦104194 the racking of her children¦104195,
\v 26 she holding two¦104197 votes, one for the death, the other for the preservation of her children,
\v 27 didn't lean to that which would have saved her children¦104212 for the safety of a brief¦104214 space.
\v 28 But¦104217 this daughter¦104223 of Avraham remembered¦104224 his holy fortitude.
\p
\v 29 Oh¦104225 holy mother¦104226 of a nation¦104227, avenger of¦104229 the¦104229 law¦104230, defender of religion, and¦104231 prime bearer in the¦104233 battle of¦104236 the¦104236 affections!
\v 30 Oh¦104241 you nobler in endurance than males, and¦104246 more courageous than men¦104247 in perseverance¦104249!
\v 31 Because like Noah's ship, bearing the¦104253 world in¦104256 the¦104257 world-filling flood, bore up against the¦104263 waves,
\v 32 so you¦104266, the¦104267 guardian of¦104268 the¦104268 law¦104268, when surrounded on every¦104269 side¦104269 by the flood of emotions, and¦104276 assaulted by violent storms which were the torments¦104283 of your children¦104282, bore up nobly against the storms against religion.
\c 16
\s1 The mother encourages her sons to remain faithful
\p
\v 1 If¦104291, then¦104293, even a woman¦104295, and¦104296 that an aged one, and¦104298 the mother¦104301 of seven¦104299 children¦104300, endured¦104302 to¦104302 see her children's¦104309 torments even to¦104312 death¦104305, it must be¦104312 admitted that religious reasoning is master even of the¦104315 emotions.
\v 2 I've proved, then¦104319, that¦104320 not only¦104322 men¦104325 have obtained the¦104323 mastery of their emotions, but¦104327 also¦104328 that a woman¦104329 despised the¦104330 greatest torments.
\v 3 The¦104337 lions around¦104338 Daniyel weren't¦104341 so¦104343 fierce, nor the furnace¦104346 of Mishael burning with most vehement fires¦104348 as that¦104353 natural¦104354 love of children burnt within her, when she saw her seven¦104358 sons¦104359 tortured¦104360.
\v 4 But¦104361 with the¦104362 reasoning of religion the¦104364 mother¦104372 quenched emotions so¦104367 great¦104367 and¦104368 powerful¦104369.
\v 5 Because we must consider also this¦104375: that¦104377, had the¦104381 woman¦104382 been faint hearted, as being their mother¦104384, she'd¦104389 have lamented over them, and¦104390 perhaps¦104391 might have spoken thus:
\v 6 “Ah! I'm¦104396 wretched and¦104399 many times miserable, who¦104402 having born seven¦104403 sons, have become the mother¦104407 of none¦104406.
\v 7 Oh¦104409 seven¦104411 useless childbirths, and¦104413 seven¦104415 profitless periods of labour, and¦104417 fruitless givings of suck, and¦104420 miserable nursings at the breast.
\v 8 Vainly¦104423, for your sakes, Oh¦104426 sons, have I endured many pangs, and¦104431 the more difficult anxieties of rearing.
\v 9 Alas, of my¦104437 children¦104438, some of you unmarried, and¦104443 some who've married to no¦104446 profit, I won't¦104450 see¦104447 your children¦104449, nor have the joy of being a grandmother¦104451.
\v 10 Ah, that I who¦104455 had many¦104456 and fair children¦104456, should be a lone widow¦104461 full of sorrows!
\v 11 Nor, should I die, will I have a son¦104470 to bury me.” But with such a lament as this, the holy and God-fearing mother wept for none of them.
\v 12 Nor did she divert any of them from death, nor grieve¦104494 for them as for the dead.
\v 13 But¦104495 as¦104496 one possessed with an adamant mind¦104500, and¦104501 as one bringing forth again her full number¦104508 of sons¦104507 to immortality¦104503, she rather urged them to¦104513 death¦104515 on behalf of religion.
\v 14 Oh¦104519 woman¦104526, soldier of¦104520 God¦104522 for religion, you, aged and¦104525 a female, have conquered through¦104527 endurance even a tyrant¦104530; and¦104532 even though weak, have been found more powerful in actions¦104533 and¦104535 words¦104536.
\v 15 Because when¦104541 you were seized along with¦104543 your children¦104545, you stood looking at Eleazar in torture¦104550, and¦104551 said to your sons in¦104555 the¦104556 Hebrew tongue,
\v 16 “O sons, the contest is noble, to¦104559 which¦104565 you being called as a witness for¦104567 the nation¦104571, strive zealously for¦104574 the laws of your country.
\v 17 Because¦104586 it'd¦104583 be disgraceful¦104579 if this¦104585 old man endured pains for the sake of¦104586 righteousness, and that¦104587 you¦104590 who¦104592 are younger would be afraid of the¦104595 tortures.
\v 18 Remember that¦104598 through¦104599 God¦104601, you obtained existence and¦104605 have enjoyed it.
\v 19 Therefore, you ought to¦104612 bear every¦104613 affliction because¦104616 of¦104616 God¦104618.
\v 20 Because¦104619 him also¦104621 our father¦104623 Avraham was zealous to¦104626 sacrifice Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add* our progenitor, and¦104632 didn't shudder at the¦104633 sight of his own paternal hand¦104635 descending down with the sword on¦104638 him.
\v 21 The¦104645 righteous Daniyel was thrown¦104649 to¦104649 the lions; and Hananyah, Azaryah, and Mishael were hurled into a fiery furnace¦104657, yet they endured¦104661 through¦104662 God¦104664.
\v 22 You¦104666, then¦104667, having the¦104668 same faith¦104670 towards God¦104673, don't¦104675 be troubled.
\v 23 Because¦104678 it's unreasonable that they who¦104683 know religion wouldn't stand up against troubles.
\v 24 With these arguments, the¦104689 mother¦104690 of¦104690 seven¦104690, exhorting each¦104692 of¦104702 her sons¦104694, encouraged and persuaded them not to transgress God's¦104703 commandment¦104701.
\v 25 They saw this¦104707, too, that¦104709 those who die¦104713 for God¦104712, live¦104714 to God¦104716, like¦104717 Avraham, Yitshak, Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add*, and all¦104724 the¦104725 patriarchs¦104726.
\c 17
\s1 The mother's death and the martyrs' reward
\p
\v 1 Some of the¦104730 spearbearers said that¦104733 when¦104734 she herself was about to be seized for the purpose of being put to¦104739 death¦104740, she threw herself on the¦104745 pile, rather than let them touch her body¦104746.
\v 2 Oh¦104753 you mother¦104754, who together with¦104755 seven¦104756 children¦104757 destroyed the¦104759 violence of¦104760 the¦104760 tyrant¦104761, and¦104763 rendered void his wicked intentions, and¦104769 exhibited the nobleness of faith¦104773!
\v 3 Because¦104789 you¦104777, like a house bravely built on¦104779 the pillar of your children¦104783, bore the¦104788 shock of tortures without swaying.
\v 4 Cheer up, therefore¦104794, Oh¦104795 holy-minded mother¦104796! Hold the¦104798 firm hope¦104799 of your steadfastness with¦104804 God¦104805.
\v 5 Not so gracious does the moon¦104808 appear with¦104811 the¦104817 stars¦104819 in heaven, as you're established as honourable¦104826 before God¦104828, and¦104829 fixed in¦104831 the sky¦104832 with your sons whom you illuminated with religion to the stars.
\v 6 Because your bearing of children was¦104835 after the¦104837 manner of¦104842 a child¦104843 of Avraham.
\p
\v 7 If¦104844 it¦104846 were lawful for us to¦104848 paint as¦104849 on a tablet the¦104852 religion of your story, the¦104853 spectators wouldn't shudder at seeing¦104861 the¦104860 mother¦104862 of seven¦104863 children¦104864 enduring for¦104865 the sake of¦104865 religion various tortures even to death¦104870.
\v 8 It'd have been¦104875 a worthwhile thing to¦104877 have inscribed on the tomb itself these words as a memorial¦104889 to those of¦104886 the¦104886 nation¦104887,
\v 9 “Here¦104891 an¦104892 aged priest¦104893, and¦104894 an aged woman, and¦104897 seven¦104898 sons, are buried through¦104901 the violence of¦104901 a tyrant¦104902, who¦104904 wished to¦104907 destroy¦104907 the society of the Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*.
\v 10 These also¦104910 avenged their nation¦104913, looking to God¦104915, and enduring torments to death¦104919.”
\v 11 Because¦104924 it was¦104925 truly¦104923 a divine¦104927 contest which was carried through¦104929 by them.
\v 12 Because¦104933 at that time virtue¦104935 presided over the contest, approving the¦104939 victory¦104940 through endurance, namely, immortality, eternal life¦104944.
\v 13 Eleazar was the first to contend. The mother of the seven children entered the contest, and the kindred contended.
\v 14 The tyrant was the antagonist; and the world and living men were the spectators.
\v 15 Reverence¦104972 for¦104972 God¦104972 conquered, and¦104973 crowned her own athletes.
\v 16 Who¦104982 didn't admire those champions of true legislation? Who weren't¦104989 amazed¦104989?
\v 17 The¦104993 tyrant¦104994 himself, and¦104995 all¦104996 their council¦104998, admired their endurance,
\v 18 through¦105004 which¦105005, they also¦105006 now¦105009 stand beside the divine throne¦105011 and¦105012 live a blessed¦105014 life.
\v 19 Because¦105018 Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* says¦105019, “All¦105023 the¦105024 saints are under¦105026 your hands¦105028.”
\v 20 These, therefore¦105032, having been sanctified through¦105034 God¦105035, have been honoured not only¦105038 with this honour¦105042, but¦105043 that also¦105044 by the¦105045 fact that because¦105046 of¦105046 them, the¦105048 enemy didn't overcome our nation¦105049.
\v 21 That tyrant was punished and their country purified.
\v 22 Because they became the ransom to the sin of the nation. The Divine Providence saved Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*, which was afflicted before, by the blood of those pious ones and the death that appeased anger.
\v 23 Because¦105090 the¦105103 tyrant¦105104 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, looking to their courageous virtue and¦105096 to¦105098 their endurance in torture¦105100, proclaimed that¦105114 endurance as an example¦105112 to his soldiers¦105109.
\v 24 They proved to be to him noble and¦105117 brave for land battles and¦105120 for sieges; and¦105124 he¦105118 conquered¦105128 and¦105126 stormed the towns of all¦105129 his enemies.
\c 18
\s1 A call to follow their example
\p
\v 1 Oh¦105132 Israeli children, descendants of the¦105133 seed¦105135 of Avraham, obey this¦105140 law¦105141 and¦105143 in every¦105144 way¦105145 be religious,
\v 2 knowing¦105147 that¦105148 religious reasoning is¦105152 lord of the¦105153 emotions, and¦105156 those not only¦105158 inward but¦105161 outward.
\p
\v 3 Therefore those people who gave up their bodies¦105173 to pains for the¦105174 sake of religion weren't only¦105178 admired by¦105179 men, but¦105183 were deemed worthy¦105187 of a divine¦105185 portion.
\v 4 The¦105192 nation¦105193 through¦105189 them obtained peace, and¦105194 having renewed the¦105195 observance of the¦105197 law in their country, drove the¦105199 enemy out of the¦105203 land.
\v 5 The¦105206 tyrant¦105207 Antiokos was both punished on¦105210 earth¦105211, and¦105213 is punished now that¦105216 he's dead; for¦105217 when he was quite unable to compel the¦105222 Israelis to adopt foreign customs, and¦105225 to desert the manner of life of their fathers,
\v 6 then, departing from Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, he made war against the Persians.
\v 7 The righteous mother of the seven children spoke also as follows to her offspring: “I was a pure virgin, and didn't go beyond my father's house, but I took care of the rib from which woman was made.
\v 8 No destroyer of the desert or ravisher of the plain injured me, nor did the destructive, deceitful snake make plunder of my chaste virginity. I remained with my husband during the time of my maturity.
\v 9 When these, my children, arrived at maturity, their father died. He was blessed! Because having sought out a life of fertility in children, he wasn't grieved with a period of loss of children.
\v 10 He used to¦105315 teach¦105315 you¦105316, when yet with¦105319 you¦105320, the¦105321 law¦105322 and¦105323 the¦105324 prophets¦105325.
\v 11 He used to¦105331 read¦105331 to you about the slaying of Hevel \add !(Abel)\add* by Kayin \add !(Cain)\add*, the offering up of Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add*, and¦105338 the¦105339 imprisonment of Yosef \add !(Joseph)\add*.
\v 12 He used to¦105343 tell¦105343 you of the¦105346 zealous Pinhas, and¦105350 informed you¦105351 of Hananyah, Azaryah, and Mishael in¦105353 the fire¦105354.
\v 13 He used to¦105360 glorify¦105360 Daniyel, who¦105363 was in¦105364 the den of lions¦105366, and¦105369 pronounce him blessed.
\v 14 He used to remind you¦105373 of the scripture of Yeshayah \add !(Isaiah)\add*, which says¦105378, “Even¦105379 if¦105379 you pass through¦105380 the fire¦105381, it won't burn¦105385 you¦105386.”
\v 15 He chanted to¦105393 you¦105390 David, the hymn writer, who says¦105393, “Many are the¦105395 afflictions of the¦105397 just¦105398.”
\v 16 He declared the¦105399 proverbs of Shelomoh \add !(Solomon)\add*, who¦105403 says¦105404, “He's a tree of life¦105406 to¦105407 all¦105408 those who¦105409 do¦105410 His will¦105413.”
\v 17 He used to¦105418 confirm what Yehezkel \add !(Ezekiel)\add* said: “Will these dry¦105424 bones live¦105420?”
\v 18 Because he didn't forget the song which Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* taught, proclaiming, “I'll kill, and I'll make alive.”
\v 19 This is our life and the length of our days.
\p
\v 20 Oh¦105450 that bitter, and yet not bitter, day¦105454 when¦105458 the¦105459 bitter¦105460 tyrant¦105462 of the Hellains \add !(Greeks)\add*, quenching fire with fire in his cruel cauldrons, brought with boiling rage the¦105479 seven¦105480 sons of the daughter of Avraham to the rack, and to all his torments!
\v 21 He pierced the balls of their eyes¦105486, and¦105489 cut out their tongues¦105490, and¦105492 put them to¦105495 death with varied tortures.
\v 22 Therefore divine retribution pursued and¦105502 will pursue the¦105504 pestilent wretch.
\v 23 But the children of Avraham, with their victorious mother¦105513, are assembled together to the choir of their father¦105515, having received pure and¦105519 immortal souls from¦105522 God¦105524.
\v 24 To¦105525 him be glory¦105527 forever and ever. Amen¦105533.
